  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... A warm and pleasant fall afternoon on tap for\nENC today as temps have warmed into the upper 60s to low 70s\nunder clear skies and steady W to NW winds behind a departing \nsurface trough. \n\nAs we've been mentioning for the past couple of days, tonight \nlooks like the best candidate so far for fairly widespread \nfrost, especially west of NC 17 as skies remain clear and winds \ninland will likely be calm with high pressure building overhead \ntonight. As is typical in these environments, deterministic \nguidance for lows is not aggressive enough, so blended in NBM \n10th percentile and the previous forecast to keep forecast lows \ninto the mid to upper 30s. Could see a few well sheltered areas \nmake a run towards the low 30s but this should be rather \nisolated. As a result a frost advisory has been issued for much\nof the Coastal Plain west of Hwy 17 tonight between midnight and\n7AM Tue. Tuesday night is forecast to remain slightly warmer as\nhigh cirrus is forecast to stream into ENC from the west \npreventing good radiational cooling Tue night limiting frost \npotential. So while it is unlikely tomorrow night it wont be \nimpossible.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Not much has changed with this weekends system\nas there remains good agreement amongst guidance for \nincreasingly unsettled weather across the eastern CONUS. A \ncomplex mid-level low will deepen over the region and steer \nmultiple shortwaves over the Mid-Atlantic. While there are still\nsome differences in exact details that will need to be hashed\nout in the coming days it remains likely that a complex low will\nsweep across the Ohio River Valley and Mid-Atlantic Wed into\nThursday with a coastal low developing off the Mid-Atlantic\ncoast Thurs into Friday with this coastal low then deepening\noffshore as it meanders around the the Northeast this weekend \nthough its intensity and track remain uncertain. Probabilistic \nguidance favors a northern track for these areas of low \npressure, which would keep heavier precipitation away from our \narea though isolated to widely scattered showers wouldn't be out\nof the question. Either way not expecting much QPF from any \nshower that impacts the area Thurs into Fri before we dry out.\n\nThe biggest threat with this aforementioned low will be the\nassociated gusty winds. In general, probabilities for a period \nof 20-30 mph W-NW winds and gusts potentially in excess of \n35-45 mph Fri into Sat remain high. If this is realized, at \nleast some coastal flooding concerns could arise soundside along\nthe Outer Banks. Given spread of guidance, it is too early to \nstate the potential magnitude of impacts. Stay tuned for future \nupdates.\n\nBehind the system, unseasonably cool air will flood into the\narea knocking highs down into the 50s for the weekend and offer\nthe first real opportunity for a freeze Sat night and Sun night.",
